  "user_impact": "Screen reader users receive no information about the image.",
  "technical_impact": "An element that is an image only through role=\"img\" (a <div>, <span> or <canvas>, often an icon or a CSS background image) has no accessible name from aria-label, aria-labelledby or title, so a screen reader announces an unnamed image; an empty aria-label is no name, and an icon inside a named link or button is no exception.",
  "checks": [
    {
      "contract_id": "images/role-img-name-missing",
      "outcome": "automatic",
      "source": "WCAG 2.2 SC 1.1.1 Non-text Content (ARIA6, ARIA10, F65); ACT 23a2a8 Image has non-empty accessible name; WAI-ARIA 1.2 img role (name required); accname 1.2",
      "applicability": "HTML elements in the accessibility tree that are an image only through role=\"img\" (Chrome's\nrole image): <div>, <span>, <canvas>, ... <img> itself is images/alt-missing, <svg> and elements\ninside it svg/name-missing, so no image element is reported by two rules. Programmatically\nhidden elements (aria-hidden, display:none, visibility:hidden) are out of scope.\nKnown limit: images generated by CSS (a pseudo-element with content: url(...), a\nbackground-image on an element without an image role) are not HTML elements with an image role,\nand no rule checks them. Whether such an image carries information (WCAG F3) needs a judgement;\nthe review hint is casoon/barrierlab#273.",
      "expectation": "Fails when the computed name is empty (ACT 23a2a8). aria-label, aria-labelledby or title pass.\nAn empty aria-label is no name and does not mark the element decorative (only role\nnone/presentation or aria-hidden do), and an image inside a named link or button is no\nexception: ACT 23a2a8 has neither.",
      "method": "shared",
      "capabilities": [
        "dom",
        "ax_tree"
      ],
      "page_level": false,
